::ENG3_U06_L08_GP01::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker presents a research-based literary claim to classmates.
Which choice best supports oral communication?{
~Begin with unrelated personal comments.#Incorrect. Unrelated comments weaken focus and do not support the claim.
=State a clear claim, use credible evidence, and organize the explanation logically.#Correct. Oral communication requires focus, credible evidence, and organization.
~Use only images and no spoken explanation.#Incorrect. Oral communication still needs clear spoken explanation.
~Avoid explaining the evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence must be explained so the audience understands the reasoning.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP02::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A digital presentation includes a chart from research.
What makes the chart effective?{
~It is colorful but unrelated.#Incorrect. Color does not help if the chart does not support the point.
~It replaces the speaker's explanation.#Incorrect. Multimedia supports explanation but does not replace it.
~It includes every detail from the research notes.#Incorrect. Too much detail can overwhelm the audience.
=It is labeled and connected to the claim being presented.#Correct. Labels and clear connection improve audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student publishes a short written summary to a class LAN page.
Which final check is most important?{
=The writing is revised, audience-aware, and supported by purposeful multimedia.#Correct. Publication readiness requires quality writing, audience fit, and integrated media.
~The page includes as many images as possible.#Incorrect. More images do not guarantee clarity.
~The writing is copied from the rough draft without review.#Incorrect. Publication requires revision.
~The product ignores reader needs.#Incorrect. Audience awareness is central to publication.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP04::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: Feedback says a paragraph has a good claim but unclear organization.
What revision best addresses the issue?{
~Change only the background color.#Incorrect. Color does not fix organization.
~Add unrelated evidence.#Incorrect. Unrelated evidence can make organization worse.
=Reorder the sentences so the claim, evidence, and explanation follow a clear path.#Correct. Revision should improve clarity and structure.
~Remove the claim entirely.#Incorrect. The claim gives the paragraph focus.
}

::ENG3_U06_L08_GP05::MLA.ENG3.L.01
Context: A caption under a presentation image has punctuation and capitalization errors.
Why should it be corrected?{
~Captions do not matter in digital work.#Incorrect. Captions are part of the communication product.
=Errors can distract the audience and weaken credibility.#Correct. Standard English conventions support clarity and trust.
~Errors make the caption more interesting.#Incorrect. Errors usually reduce clarity.
~The image replaces the caption completely.#Incorrect. Captions often explain how media supports the message.
}
